/*  carrierT -- THE PARSE PARKS IN builtinParseR AND THE RULE KEEPS ITS ACTION.

    Station 4's landing, 2026-09-18. `generateParse` used to write the generated parse into
    the rule's own CodE -- which is where a rule with a code body keeps its ACTION -- so
    generating a parse DELETED the action. That was fixIts F-87. A rule that already carries
    an action body now gets its parse parked in a noPrint `builtinParseR` attribute, in
    builtinActoR's shape, and CodE is left alone.

    TWO OTHER THINGS LANDED WITH IT AND BOTH ARE LOAD-BEARING, each measured as a
    one-variable A/B:
      walkRules skips noPrint   CodE reads isRulE=1 AND noPrinT=1, so the isRulE test alone
                                let the walk descend INTO THE ACTION BODY and generate a parse
                                for it. compile() has always skipped noPrint first; this is the
                                same discipline, and it skips builtinParseR too.
      hasNewParse withheld      when the parse parks in the carrier. That flag tells runRule A
                                PARSE IS INSTALLED AND FIRABLE, not merely that one was
                                generated -- one channel, two meanings. Raised over a parked
                                carrier it is a promise nothing keeps: runRule takes the gMethod
                                arm and SPINS. That was fixIts F-88, and it closes here.
                                A/B: withheld, exit 0 and the action fires; raised, exit 142 on
                                a 45-second alarm with no output at all.

    THE ROWS
      CT-1  the driver ran -- the anti-vacuity sibling for everything below
      CT-2  THE ACTION FIRES after parser(list). This is F-87 closed, and before this stroke
            it did not fire at all.
      CT-3  `sumGrup` is still in list's CodE. THE ACTION BODY ITSELF survived, by a name only
            that body declares -- presence with value, not "CodE is still there".
      CT-4  builtinParseR is ON the rule. The parse was generated and parked, not discarded --
            CT-2 alone would also pass if generation had simply stopped happening.
      CT-5  the body WALKS ITS MATCHED TERMS: the for loop prints every matched entry, and the
            row asserts the last one. Re-pinned 2026-10-02 (SEQ 260, deepClean D-2) from a
            deliberate wrong pin on F-83's label gap, which label population closed. It is also
            the row that proves CT-2 reached the loop rather than stopping at its first statement.
      CT-6  exit 0. The spin is gone, and a spin cannot be asserted by a diff -- a killed
            process yields truncated output and names the wrong row.  */
Start();
include(unitTests);
include(utilities);
search reset stack Grokking;
search GroupFields UnitTests Utilities list;

register(CarrierPark);
define
    ctOk=0;
    ctMiss=0;
    ctPark code={
        ctOk = 0;
        if list["builtinParseR"];       ctOk = 1;
        ctMiss = 0;
        if list["zzzNotAnArtifact"];    ctMiss = 1;
        print "CT-4 carrier present " ctOk " miss control " ctMiss:;
        };
    ;
parser(list);
testList();
ctPark();

cerr "CT-4 builtinParseR on list":;
dumpContents(list);
cerr "CT-3 list CodE":;
dumpContents(list["CodE"]);

cerr "CARRIER SENTINEL":;
stop();
